WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.020
Forbes once called her “The Warren Buffett
of vampires,” but American author, Anne

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.590
Rice has established herself as the literary
queen of monsters of ALL kinds over her four-and-a-half

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:10.590
decade career.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.360
Besides her 15 novels of the world-famous
Vampire Chronicles series, she’s also written

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.509
21 other books featuring all your favorite
dark, supernatural, and undead beings: witches,

00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:28.009
ghosts, mummies, werewolves, aliens, demons,
angels, Jesus.

00:00:28.009 --> 00:00:33.079
But the works of Anne rice aren't just light,
pulpy fun monster books--her vampires changed

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:39.660
the landscape of genre fiction as we know
it.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.180
Rice calls her novels “gothic epics.”

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.800
Her groundbreaking genre-blending style combines
literary fundamentals with dark supernatural

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.910
elements and fantasy tropes.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:54.079
Some are sweeping historical epics or ancestral
family sagas, while others lean much towards

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.920
the action/adventure category.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.860
The prose can go from exceedingly indulgently
purple to short and choppy noir style, sometimes

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.609
even in the same book.

00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:08.630
In 1968, Anne Rice penned a short story about
a vampire… wait for it... being interviewed,

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.740
but she wasn’t having much luck establishing
a literary career.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.690
As she pursued a masters’ degree in creative
writing, she found herself frustrated with

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.740
the repeated advice from her mentors that
if she ever wanted to be successful, she needed

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.440
to write “serious” literature about typical
middle class people in everyday life.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.560
Said Rice: “Anything other than stories
of ordinary people was considered "fantasy,"

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.460
or science fiction or perhaps "romance" and
decidedly of lesser value […] Such genre

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.370
fiction could not hope to be "mainstream.”

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.939
Despite this discouragement, Rice kept being
drawn to the supernatural and monsters.

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:44.440
But she didn’t find her calling until she
tied those monsters in with her own personal

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.440
tragedies.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.030
While grieving for the death of her 5 year
old daughter to leukemia in 1972, Rice sank

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:55.030
herself into her writing, and she revisited
that old short story about the vampire.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:02:00.640
She added in a tragic little girl vampire
character and finished her first novel, 1976’s

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.229
Interview With the Vampire--the life story
of the melancholy, reluctant vampire, Louis

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:07.680
and his extremely gay vampire family.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.410
But the journey to publishing was a struggle
because the book didn’t fit into any established

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:15.410
genre--It was a fantasy, but ALSO “serious.”

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:20.060
Its prose and style read like literary fiction,
yet it featured supernatural characters and

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.060
events.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.330
It’s perhaps because of that that Interview
ended up being a literary sensation that shocked

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:26.520
publishers.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.090
She followed up Interview with two historical
novels that had absolutely no supernatural

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.690
elements at all ….And found out the hard
way that historical fiction was looked down

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:39.019
upon by all those “serious” snobs just
as much as fantasy and romance.

00:02:39.019 --> 00:02:42.629
So she decided that if she was going to be
pigeonholed, she might as well follow her

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:43.629
passion.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:47.831
She spent some time writing erotica under
pseudonyms--some supernatural, some not, and

00:02:47.831 --> 00:02:51.420
through that found the bravery to go back
to writing about the dark themes she had always

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:57.190
been drawn to with vampires. ...and witches
and ghosts and mummies and aliens and werewolves…

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:58.520
and Jesus.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.069
In 1985, The Vampire Lestat was released.

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:05.129
A sequel to Interview with the Vampire, it
tells the life story of Lestat, a major supporting

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:09.739
character from Interview, who thenceforth
took over as the series’ hero.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:10.739
“Hero?”

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:11.739
Hero-ish.

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:15.300
This time, the critical reaction was much
more positive, and the Vampire Chronicles

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.659
was born!

00:03:16.659 --> 00:03:20.410
And continues to thrive--The fifteenth book
in the series, Blood Communion: A Tale of

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.250
Prince Lestat, just came out in 2018.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.250
Says Rice.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.349
"On vampires, they are the most powerful metaphor
for the outsider I ever encountered.”

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:32.599
As her books became worldwide bestsellers,
the outcasts Anne Rice wrote about resonated

00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:37.150
widely with audiences who saw themselves depicted
in the characters despite their supernatural

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.150
natures.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:39.890
Or maybe--because of them?

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.830
Again, rice: “[Vampires are] great metaphors
for us because secretly we ALL feel a bit

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:45.830
like a monster.

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.800
We’re all predators in our own way.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.569
We’re ALL outcast to some extent.”

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:55.409
And it’s easy to forget in our current vampirescape,
which ranges from zombie demons to sparkly

00:03:55.409 --> 00:04:02.900
heroes in romances to loving fathers in family
films to comic leads in mockumentaries to…

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:09.470
whatever this is, before Anne Rice, vampires
were rarely to be sympathized with.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:13.470
Like all the various kinds of “monsters”
in Rice’s books, the vampires feature as

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:17.660
the protagonists, and are sympathetic and
complex characters despite being murderous

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.290
creatures of the night--these are no “vegetarian
vampires.”

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:25.730
And the stories themselves don’t follow
the classic human vs monster conflict but

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.640
instead are monster vs monster.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.380
Monster WITH FEELINGS vs monster with different
feelings, having ideological differences and

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.280
conflicts of morality.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.670
Says David Punter and Glennis Byron in their
book, The Gothic: “Interview with the Vampire

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:44.800
[...] offers one of the most significant rewritings
of the traditional myth since it was established

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.810
by Bram Stoker in Dracula.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.610
Shifting the narrative perspective to the
vampire himself, [...], the text begins to

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:54.660
dissolve the conventional boundaries between
the vampire and the human.”

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:58.690
And says Milly Williamson, in her book, The
Lure of the Vampire: “[the vampire] is no

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.780
longer predominantly a figure of fear in Western
popular culture, but a figure of sympathy.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:08.800
Thus, rather than a manifestation of our ‘grisly
nightmares,’ [...] the vampire today speaks

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.980
instead to our undead desires”.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:17.330
This sympathetic, complex, sensitive, and
sexy way Rice wrote vampires was hugely inspirational

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:18.950
for the vampire genre.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:23.220
Many authors and filmmakers that came after
were directly influenced by her specific vampire

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.550
mythology and philosophies.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.640
The literary vampire can be used as an allegory
for just about every social/sociopolitical

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.220
issue you can think of -- After Rice, vampirism
became a metaphor for such varied topics as

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:40.890
drug addiction, homosexuality, AIDS, and the
general selfishness and narcissism of the

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.690
baby boomer generation.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:47.380
But one of Anne Rice’s biggest recurring
themes is the use of the supernatural as an

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.050
exploration of Grief.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:52.990
She started off using writing as catharsis
for losing her daughter and processing the

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:56.900
trauma of her alcoholic mother dying when
she was a teen, as well as her own general

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.500
feelings of alienation from society.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.930
Religion is also a major recurring theme in
Rice’s work.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.600
Raised Catholic, Anne Rice became an atheist
at the age of 18.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.251
She returned to the Catholic church in 1998
only to reject it again a decade later, and

00:06:10.251 --> 00:06:15.120
the characters in her books often reflect
her complicated feelings about her own spirituality.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.370
We see the resolute atheist, Lestat, return
to religion himself in 2003’s Blood Canticle

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:24.440
when Rice temporarily rejoined the church,
but he’s back to his old ways by the next

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:27.440
time he appears in 2014’s Prince Lestat.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.560
The early vampire chronicles especially feature
the clash between atheism and spirituality

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.560
in the moral approaches the different vampires
take to reconcile their dark existences.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:39.870
They’re all killers, but they’re often
super sad and philosophical about it.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:43.970
Characters with catholic hangups like the
original reluctant vampire himself, Louis,

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:48.800
living in guilt and self loathing, butt heads
against atheists like Lestat, whose joi de

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:53.380
vivre gives him the strength to bounce back
from every tragedy that happens to him.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:57.440
Says psychology professor and author Katherine
Ramsland, “[Louis] is impelled to search

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.020
for answers to the ultimate questions of life,
and is especially concerned to discover whether

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:06.250
God exists, and if so, if that makes him a
child of the devil’.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:10.170
Another theme that rises throughout Anne Rice’s
work is that of the sensitive, lonely monster

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:14.390
seeking connection with others of their kind
as a metaphor for the queer experience.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:17.480
I mean this isn’t me putting on my shipping
glasses.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:21.130
Nina Auerbach makes the important point that
“the fraught menage of Louis and Lestat

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.450
is a return to vampire beginnings.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:28.400
Their irritable mutual obsession recovers
literary vampires’ lost origin: the homoerotic

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.140
bond between [Lord] Byron and Polidori”.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:36.250
Ah yes, the original 19th century frenemies
who gave us the very first literary vampire.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.360
Isn’t it Byronic.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:42.160
Even though her vampires do not have sexual
intercourse, in Interview with the Vampire,

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.220
Louis and Lestat are “clearly married to
each other” and adopt and raise a daughter

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:47.220
together.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.690
The series also features many other explicitly
queer vampires who are attracted to all genders,

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.590
both when in seach of a meal and romance.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:58.900
This pansexual attraction is just something
that happens to people when they become vampires,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:03.460
regardless of their orientations in life:
“I think the imagination is bisexual,”

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.460
Rice says.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:08.330
“Once you're 'out of nature,' to use Yeats's
phrase, you see all people as beautiful and

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:13.050
you make a bond to people of your own sex
as easily as to people of the opposite sex.'

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.050
[... ]”

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:18.440
Witches, ghosts, mummies, werewolves, aliens,
vampires… so...serious!

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.580
And now, besides its popular success continually
reaching the NYT Best Sellers lists, Anne

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:27.240
Rice’s work is taught in universities and
referenced by academics the world over.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.890
“What a wonderful thing,” she says, “to
have lived long enough to see the power of

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:35.909
labels broken, the "rules" of genre thrown
to the winds, the bias of high culture ignored

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:37.030
or stood on its head.”

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:40.590
Except she didn’t just live long enough
to see it, she was instrumental in making

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:41.650
it happen.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:46.260
Her relatable and attractive monsters changed
the landscape of supernatural literature.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.970
And she accomplished it through sheer shamelessness.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:53.590
She was bold enough to write her way about
her grief, her spiritual struggles, her fascination

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.930
with gender, and so much else.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:00.360
And that vulnerability spoke to people, especially
those who felt like outcasts, who felt like

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.070
they weren’t allowed to be themselves in
the world:

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:07.500
“I had to follow my own eccentric path,
creating novels that no one could classify

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:08.500
or explain.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:12.960
I craved the spectacle of historical setting,
and the agony of tragic characters [...] And

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.670
if my career proves anything, it is that if
a person fervently believes in her own vision,

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:22.000
no matter how eccentric or weird, well, somebody
else may believe in it too.”

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.930
And with almost 100 million copies of her
books sold worldwide, making her one of the

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.330
most widely read authors in modern history...

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.260
yes, it turns out somebody did.

